{"model":"Qwen3.8 Flash Next","scenario":"chat","layers":48,"experts":512,"topK":10,"promptTokens":36,"prefillTokens":35,"prefillSeconds":2.56,"decodeSeconds":25.46,"steps":300,"distinctExpertsPerLayer":[390,371,363,253,258,298,355,310,256,205,271,260,292,293,206,118,156,222,309,242,240,295,332,183,249,203,253,239,275,254,198,101,139,217,284,226,205,261,308,146,170,223,270,200,217,231,236,261],"text":"The main differences between **TCP (Transmission Control Protocol)** and **UDP (User Datagram Protocol)** lie in their approach to reliability, speed, and connection management. Both are transport layer protocols used in the Internet Protocol Suite (TCP/IP), but they serve different purposes.\n\n### Key Differences\n\n| Feature | TCP | UDP |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Connection Type** | **Connection-oriented**: Establishes a connection via a 3-way handshake before data transfer. | **Connectionless**: Sends data without establishing a connection first. |\n| **Reliability** | **Reliable**: Guarantees delivery, order, and error-checking. Retransmits lost packets. | **Unreliable**: No guarantee of delivery, order, or error-checking. Lost packets are simply dropped. |\n| **Speed** | **Slower**: Overhead from handshakes, acknowledgments, and retransmissions. | **Faster**: Minimal overhead; low latency. |\n| **Data Flow** | **Stream-oriented**: Data is sent as a continuous stream of bytes. | **Message-oriented**: Data is sent in discrete packets (datagrams). |\n| **Header Size** | Larger header (20 bytes minimum). | Smaller header (8 bytes). |\n| **Congestion Control** | Yes: Adjusts transmission rate based on network congestion. | No: Sends data at a constant rate regardless of network conditions.","tokens":[{"id":271,"pos":35,"turn":0,"phase":"last-prompt"},{"id":760,"pos":36,"turn":0,"phase":"decode"},{"id":1822,"pos":37,"turn":0,"phase":"decode"},{"id":11461,"pos":38,"turn":0,"phase":"decode"},{"id":1881,"pos":39,"turn":0,"phase":"decode"},{"id":2972,"pos":40,"turn":0,